{
  "policy_id": "ld-106-freeze-rail-fares-and-simplify-ticketing",
  "party": "Liberal Democrat",
  "policy_title": "Freeze Rail Fares and Simplify Ticketing",
  "outcome": "O6",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O6",
    "direction": "improves",
    "magnitude": "minor",
    "time_horizon": "long-term",
    "confidence": "low",
    "plain": "Cheaper rail fares could nudge some people from cars to trains, which would cut emissions \u2014 but the actual shift in travel behaviour is uncertain and the environmental gain is likely small relative to total transport emissions. The long-term benefit depends on whether lower fares genuinely bring new passengers rather than just benefiting existing ones.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether the fare freeze triggers meaningful modal shift from car to rail at a scale that materially reduces transport emissions, or mainly benefits existing rail users.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy aims to make rail an environmentally-friendly option for passengers and freight.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "Make rail a genuinely convenient, affordable and environmentally-friendly option for both passengers and freight"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Cars currently account for 84% of non-walking journeys in the UK, indicating the scale of potential modal shift.",
        "evidence_id": "E13",
        "quote": "cars account for 84% of non-walking journeys in the UK, highlighting the potential environmental benefit of increased rail use"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The Department for Transport anticipates the fare freeze will stimulate new journeys.",
        "evidence_id": "E12",
        "quote": "The Department for Transport (DfT) anticipates that the fare freeze will stimulate new journeys, which could lead to a lower net cost for the policy than the headline passenger savings"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Rail fares have grown 34% between 2000-01 and 2023-24, outpacing income growth, suggesting cost has been a barrier.",
        "evidence_id": "E20",
        "quote": "rail fares growing 34% between 2000-01 and 2023-24"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The long-term sustainability of the rail funding model is uncertain, with questions about how the funding gap will be bridged alongside investments in modernisation.",
        "evidence_id": "E27",
        "quote": "the need for clarity from the Treasury on how the funding gap created by the fare freeze will be bridged, alongside investments in modernization and punctuality"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "The environmental case for this policy rests on modal shift: if lower fares attract car users onto trains, transport emissions fall. The baseline evidence is clear \u2014 cars dominate UK surface travel at 84% of non-walking journeys, so the headroom for shift is large in principle. Rail fares have risen sharply over two decades, and cost is a plausible barrier. The DfT projects that the freeze will stimulate new journeys, which would deliver some environmental benefit. However, this is a projected, not measured, effect. No cited evidence quantifies how many new passengers would be car switchers versus induced demand or people switching from other low-emission modes (walking, cycling, bus). The policy also primarily benefits existing regulated-fare rail users (the savings data in E2\u2013E5 describe existing commuters), who are already not driving. The environmental gain is therefore likely to be real but modest and slow-building \u2014 a long-term marginal improvement rather than a transformative shift. The Railway Agency / GBR reform could compound the benefit by making rail more attractive, but that mechanism is separate and institutional. On balance, the direction is a modest long-term improvement for O6, driven by plausible but unquantified modal-shift effects, with low confidence because no cited evidence establishes the scale of emissions reduction.",
    "time_split": {
      "near_direction": "negligible",
      "long_direction": "improves",
      "note": "Near-term, the freeze mainly benefits existing rail users with little immediate modal shift; environmental gains accrue gradually as sustained lower fares and simplification draw car users to rail over time."
    },
